ios: touch model (one gesture per touch, forgiving slots, momentum, haptics)

Every touch is now exactly one of: a tap, a long-press, a camera turn, an item or scrollbar drag, or a scroll.

- No dead band: the camera turn starts at TOUCH_SLOP (14 px; upstream 20), and its first step turns by the whole way
  from where the finger went down, so no movement is lost to the threshold. A touch released after moving past the
  slop without becoming a turn, drag or scroll never clicks: upstream clicked where the finger lifted, so a long-press
  that drifted 12-18 px walked or attacked ("Attack Man") instead of opening the menu, and a short drag on the world
  chopped a tree instead of turning the camera.
- A long-press opens a menu only where there is something to choose (at least one option besides "Cancel"): on the
  minimap or bare panel stone it used to right-click into nothing or into a "Cancel"-only menu, with a haptic. Such a
  hold is a tap when it lifts. The client no longer plays the long-press haptic itself: the host plays it when it
  actually shows the menu ('menu-open'). hudState().longPressable tells the host whether a long-press at the pointer
  would open a menu (for its hold indicator).
- In game, a tap that is not on an input area while the native keyboard is up only hides the keyboard, like a tap
  beside the keyboard anywhere in iOS; it used to also walk, attack or open what it landed on. An open menu still
  takes the tap. The title screen is unchanged.
- On a touch screen an inventory slot takes its whole grid cell (the 32x32 icon plus half the margins around it,
  42x36 in the inventory), for its options, long-press menus, drags and drops. A tap or a drop a few px beside an icon
  gave only "Cancel". Exactly one slot takes a point: where slots have offsets of their own, the cells overlap (worn
  equipment: icons 41 px apart in 56 px cells, so a tap on the cape's right edge removed the amulet), and the slot
  whose icon is under the finger takes it, else the one whose icon is nearest. Nothing is drawn differently.
- Momentum: a drag-to-scroll (patch "drag-to-scroll") glides on after the finger lifts, at the finger's speed over its
  last 80 ms, slowing by 0.9 per client cycle (20 ms) until under 0.5 px per cycle, stopping at the end of the list,
  when its interface closes, or at the next touch. As on a native list, a tap on the list while it still glides (at
  1 px per cycle or more) only stops it; it used to also run the row under the finger (a quest journal, the bank's
  Withdraw-1). A tap elsewhere acts as usual, and a right click (right-click mode, a starved long-press) still opens
  the menu.
- Feedback: a finger pressing an interface button (IF_BUTTON, toggle, select, close, "Click here to continue") or one
  of the title screen's buttons gets the 'selection' haptic of a native button. Clicks by a mouse or by the host
  (tapAt) do not.

